[2026-08-02T14:33:22Z]
Here is the content of the link


This is a copy of a chat between Claude and Luc. Content may include unverified or unsafe content that do not represent the views of Anthropic. Shared snapshot may contain attachments and data not displayed here.

I would like to create my own lora protocol. It is encrypted (AES128) and has only 1 group. There is some repeater / bridge that allows to communicate using serial. TTL is by default 2 and max 7, there is max 255 nodes and we can either flood or send to a specific node.  We could think about a table containing for each node the last  counter to prevent issue with repetition. How would you design such a new protocol so that it is small and reliable. For targetted instruction we would need a confirmation. We could think about direct or flood protocol if we know there is a direct path no need to repeat the instruction.

Here's a design I'd propose. The core ideas: an 8-bit address space, a per-source monotonic counter that does double duty as anti-replay and AES nonce, a mutable hop byte kept outside the MAC, and an escalation ladder from direct → flood.

Frame layout
Overhead is 7 bytes of header + 4 bytes of tag = 11 bytes on top of your payload.



Crypto
Use AES-128-CCM (CCM* with M=4), one primitive, available in mbedtls/tinycrypt/ESP-IDF. Not raw AES-CTR + separate CMAC unless you want LoRaWAN-style key separation.

Nonce (13 B): src(1) ‖ counter(3) ‖ dst(1) ‖ ctrl(1) ‖ 0x00 × 7. Uniqueness is guaranteed by (src, counter) never repeating, which is exactly the property you also need for replay protection — one counter, two jobs.
AAD: ctrl ‖ src ‖ dst ‖ counter (bytes 0–5). hop is deliberately excluded so relays can decrement it.
Plaintext: type(1) ‖ body. Keeping the type encrypted costs nothing and gives you 256 message types without a header field.
The TTL-tampering hole that mutable fields normally open is closed two ways: ttl_initial is inside the AAD, so the final receiver checks the invariant ttl_remaining + hops_taken == ttl_initial and drops anything inconsistent; and an attacker replaying with a bumped TTL is caught by the dedup table below.

Relays must verify the MIC before forwarding. This is the single biggest reliability win: only authentic group traffic ever gets amplified, so a noisy neighbour or a jammer can't make your mesh flood itself.

Counters and the dedup table
Transmit 24 bits (16.7M messages — never wraps at LoRa duty cycles, so no rollover-resync logic, unlike LoRaWAN's 16-bit FCnt). Per node you keep:

c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t last_cnt;      // highest counter seen
  uint32_t window;        // 32-bit bitmap, IPsec-style anti-replay
  int8_t   last_snr;
  uint8_t  hops;          // 0 = direct neighbour
  uint32_t last_heard;
} peer_t;                 // ~14 B × 255 = ~3.5 kB
The sliding window matters because with flooding the same frame arrives via several paths and out of order. Accept if cnt > last_cnt (shift window) or if it's within the window and the bit is clear. Otherwise drop and don't relay — this is your flood-suppression mechanism and your replay defence in one structure.

On tiny nodes, allocate lazily: only peers you've actually heard.

Reboot handling. Your own TX counter must survive power loss or you reuse a CCM nonce. Persist it to flash with a forward jump (cnt += 1000 on boot, write every 1000 sends) — that's ~16k flash writes over the device lifetime, fine for any MCU. Forward jumps are always safe for receivers; only backward movement breaks things.

Direct vs flood
Any received frame with hops_taken == 0 marks that source as a direct neighbour. With normal traffic you barely need beacons — but a HELLO broadcast every 10–15 min (flood=0, ttl=0, ~12 byte frame) keeps the table warm on quiet networks.

Escalation ladder for a targeted command:

Attempt	Flags	TTL	Timeout
1–2	flood=0, ack_req=1	0	2 × airtime + 200 ms
3–4	flood=1, ack_req=1	2	2 × (2h+1) × airtime
5	flood=1, ack_req=1	4	proportional
Skip straight to step 3 if the destination isn't a fresh direct neighbour.

ACK: type=0x20, body = acked_cnt(3) ‖ status(1). 16-byte frame. Set the ACK's ttl_initial to the hops_taken of the request you received — exactly enough hops to get back, no more. If the request came in at hops_taken == 0, reply direct.

Never ACK broadcasts (dst=0xFF) — that's an ACK storm at 255 nodes. If you need confirmed broadcast, poll nodes individually afterwards.

Relay behaviour
Three rules, in order:

Dedup — if (src, cnt) is already in the window, drop silently.
Jitter — wait a random 0 … 3 × airtime before relaying. Without this every relay in range transmits simultaneously and they all collide.
Overhear suppression — if during the jitter window you hear the same (src, cnt) relayed by ≥2 other nodes, cancel your own relay. This is a cheap Trickle/MPR approximation and it's what keeps a flood from being N² transmissions.
Then decrement ttl_remaining, increment hops_taken, drop if TTL hits 0, retransmit. The relay is otherwise stateless — no route tables, no next-hop, no per-hop ACKs. End-to-end ACK only.

Airtime (why this matters more than byte count)
EU868, BW125, CR 4/5, 16-byte frame:

SF	Airtime	Command + ACK, 2 relays
7	~51 ms	~0.3 s
9	~165 ms	~1 s
12	~1.3 s	~8 s
At SF12 the 1% duty cycle limit bites hard: one frame costs you 130 s of silence. Cap application payload around 40–50 bytes and prefer SF7–SF9 for anything interactive. Shaving your header from 7 to 6 bytes changes airtime by roughly one symbol; getting flood suppression right changes it by an order of magnitude.

Serial bridge
Keep the key on the bridge, not the host. That way the bridge can relay autonomously even if the host is down, and the key never touches a Linux filesystem. Expose plaintext over serial.

A line-oriented ASCII protocol is worth the few extra bytes for how much easier it makes debugging with screen/socat:

> TX 0x2A F=DA C=1 484921        # to node 42, direct+ack, hex payload
< OK 0x00A41F                     # accepted, assigned counter
< ACK 0x00A41F 00                 # confirmed, status 0
< RX 0x11 -92 7.5 h1 48656C6C6F   # from 17, rssi, snr, 1 hop
< TIMEOUT 0x00A41F
Add STAT (dump the peer table), SF/PWR (radio config) and a RAW mode that dumps encrypted frames for sniffing. If you'd rather push crypto to the host, use COBS framing with a CRC16 instead — but then the bridge can't dedup or relay on its own.

Things I'd leave out
No fragmentation (cap the payload instead), no hop-by-hop ACK, no routing protocol, no source routing. At 255 nodes with TTL ≤ 7, controlled flooding with a good suppression heuristic beats anything table-driven, and it degrades gracefully when nodes move or die.

The one extension worth planning for: a rsv bit in ctrl to signal "extended header follows", so you can add a relay-ID byte or a 2-hop neighbour bitmap later without breaking the version-0 parser.

what is mic

MIC = Message Integrity Code — an authentication tag. It's the same idea as a MAC (Message Authentication Code); LoRaWAN and 802.15.4 call it "MIC" to avoid confusion with "MAC address" and "MAC layer", which is why I used that name here.

What it does: it's a cryptographic checksum computed over the frame with the shared AES-128 key. When CCM encrypts your payload, it also produces this tag. On reception you recompute it — if it doesn't match byte for byte, you discard the frame.

Two properties that a CRC can't give you:

Authenticity. Only someone holding the group key can produce a valid tag. An attacker who captures a frame, flips a bit in the destination address or the payload, and retransmits it will produce a frame whose MIC no longer verifies. This is what lets your relays refuse to amplify injected traffic.
Integrity over the ciphertext and the cleartext header. That's the AAD part — ctrl ‖ src ‖ dst ‖ counter isn't encrypted (relays need to read it), but it is covered by the tag, so it can't be altered undetected. Only the hop byte sits outside, deliberately.
Why 4 bytes. The tag is truncated from AES's 16-byte block. Four bytes means a blind forgery succeeds with probability 2⁻³², so an attacker needs ~4 billion attempts on average. At LoRa duty cycles that's centuries of transmission, so 4 bytes is the right trade against airtime. LoRaWAN uses exactly this. If you were doing something safety-critical you'd go to 8 bytes; the cost is one extra symbol of airtime at SF7.

Note this is separate from the LoRa PHY CRC, which you should also leave enabled. The PHY CRC catches random radio corruption cheaply, before you spend CPU on decryption. The MIC catches deliberate tampering. A frame has to pass both.

Not sure if we need hop, we could use the ctrl byte to reduce the number available. I would also like in the ctrl the kind of packet.  And I would also like to store if the counter is on 3 or 4 bytes. For version we could have only 1 bit, for flood we could use as address 255. Could you check and repropose a structure ?

Good instincts — dropping the hop byte and moving the type into ctrl nets you two bytes, because the type no longer needs the first plaintext byte either. Overhead goes from 12 to 10 bytes. But two of the four changes have consequences worth naming before the layout.

Two things to settle first
dst = 0xFF cannot be your only flood signal. Broadcast and relay-allowed are orthogonal. A targeted command to a distant node is unicast and needs relaying; a HELLO beacon is broadcast and must not be relayed. Using 0xFF for the flag conflates them.

The fix is free: ttl == 0 means "do not relay". That's what a countdown gives you naturally. dst=0xFF then just means "everyone processes this", and ttl independently controls range. Your direct-vs-flood decision becomes ttl=0 vs ttl=2, no flag bit needed.

Losing the hop byte costs you per-packet hop count. You had hops_taken == 0 as a free neighbour detector on every frame. With one 3-bit countdown you can't recover it — the receiver doesn't know the initial value.

I think that's fine, because your escalation ladder already answers the question: if a ttl=0 attempt gets an ACK, the peer is a direct neighbour; if it fails after retries, it isn't. HELLO beacons at ttl=0 keep the table warm on quiet links. You lose passive neighbour discovery from ordinary traffic, which mostly means the table converges slower on a network that's otherwise silent.

Proposed layout


c
#define TTL_MASK   0x07
#define CTRL_IMM   0xF8          // immutable bits

uint8_t ctrl = (ver << 7) | (type << 4) | (cntsz << 3) | ttl;
Types (3 bits)
Val	Type	Ack?	Body
0	HELLO	no	empty, always ttl=0
1	DATA	no	payload
2	DATA_ACK	yes	payload
3	ACK	—	cnt[23:0](3) ‖ status(1)
4	CMD	yes	payload
5	RESP	no	payload
6	NACK	—	cnt[23:0](3) ‖ reason(1)
7	EXT	—	extended ctrl in first plaintext byte
Ack-request folds into the type, which is why you don't need a separate bit. ACK echoes only the low 24 bits of the counter — collisions within a retry window are impossible, and it keeps the ACK body fixed at 4 bytes regardless of the originator's counter size. Total ACK frame: 14 bytes.

Crypto, adjusted for the mutable ctrl
CCM's AAD is byte-granular, so you can't authenticate half a byte. The trick is to mask:

c
nonce[13] = { ctrl & CTRL_IMM, src, dst,
              cnt>>24, cnt>>16, cnt>>8, cnt,   // always 4 B, zero-extended
              0,0,0,0,0,0 };
aad       = { ctrl & CTRL_IMM, src, dst, <counter as transmitted> };
Every immutable bit — version, type, counter size — is still cryptographically bound. Only the 3 TTL bits are malleable, which is unavoidable for any hop-limited protocol.

You do lose the ttl_initial + hops == ttl_remaining cross-check I had before, so an attacker replaying a captured frame with TTL bumped to 7 is no longer detectable at the endpoint. Two defences: the dedup window catches it as soon as the legitimate copy has been seen, and — worth adding — relays enforce a local acceptance cap, refusing to forward anything arriving with ttl > MAX_ACCEPT (3 is a sane default). That bounds amplification to something you configure, independent of what the sender or an attacker claims.

The counter-size bit
Make it self-describing rather than configured, otherwise you have a per-node setting that can drift:

A node uses cntsz=0 while its counter is below 2²⁴, and switches permanently to cntsz=1 at that point.

Two properties fall out of this. The nonce is always built from the zero-extended 32-bit value, so the 3→4 byte transition can never produce a nonce collision. And the peer table stores uint32_t unconditionally, so the receiver needs no special handling — it just widens what it read.

At one message per minute a node reaches 2²⁴ after 32 years, so in practice only a bridge or gateway ever flips. That's exactly the asymmetry the bit is worth having for.

The 1-bit version field
One bit gives you exactly one future revision, which isn't much of an upgrade path. I'd spend it differently: define ver=0 as this protocol, and ver=1 as "an extended ctrl byte follows the header" — a 7-byte header where the second byte carries a full 8-bit version plus whatever flags v2 needs. You keep 1 bit today and get 256 versions later, at the cost of one byte only on frames that need it.

type=7 (EXT) gives you the same escape hatch from the other direction, for options that belong in the encrypted region.

Resulting sizes
Frame	Bytes
HELLO	10
ACK	14
CMD, 16 B payload	26
Max useful payload	~40 (recommended cap, not the 245 the PHY allows)
At SF7 a 26-byte command is roughly 62 ms, so a command plus ACK across two relays lands near 0.4 s.

could / should all the nodes remember the last counter for each node so that they can either not repeat or just ignore the packet ? There is still a risk to resent. a packet and that a new device is not aware of the current counter of each device. Could / should a new device receive the current status of all the counter ?

Yes — but with an important distinction between who needs the table and what it protects, because the answers differ.

Every node needs it, for two different reasons
Relaying nodes need it for flood suppression. Without dedup, a frame arriving via three paths gets retransmitted three times by every relay, and you get the N² storm.

Leaf nodes need it to avoid acting twice on a command that reached them via two paths. That's not a security property, it's basic correctness.

So yes, universal. The cost is bounded: allocate lazily and only for peers you actually hear. A node in a corner of the network with four neighbours holds four entries, not 255.

The retry rule that makes this work
Worth stating explicitly because it's easy to get wrong: a retransmission must reuse the same counter. If a CMD gets no ACK, you resend the identical frame with the identical counter — you do not increment. Otherwise the receiver treats it as a new command and executes twice, because it can't tell "the ACK was lost" from "he really sent it again".

A pleasant consequence of masking the TTL bits out of the nonce: escalating from ttl=0 to ttl=2 leaves the nonce and AAD unchanged, so the ciphertext and MIC are byte-identical. You encrypt once, cache the result, and each retry just rewrites one byte. No re-encryption on the escalation ladder.

Rule: same payload → same counter. Different payload → new counter.

The cold-start hole is real, and narrow
You've identified it correctly. A node with no entry for peer P will accept the first frame it sees from P at face value. An attacker who captured CMD unlock last month can replay it exactly once into a freshly provisioned node. After that the window slams shut.

But note this isn't only about new devices. It's the general "no state for this peer" path, which you also hit after a factory reset, after a crash if you didn't persist the table, and after evicting a rarely-heard peer from a lazily-allocated table. Treating it as an onboarding special case will leave holes.

Don't bulk-transfer counters — challenge instead
A snapshot has a bootstrapping problem: an attacker can replay an old snapshot, seeding you with counters that are too low and reopening exactly the window you were trying to close. To fix that you need freshness on the snapshot, which is the same problem one level up. It's also expensive — 255 entries at 4 bytes is 16 frames.

The cheap primitive is a nonce the attacker cannot have precomputed:



Two new types, using your EXT escape or two of the spare slots:

Type	Body	Note
CHAL	nonce(4)	receiver → sender, ttl mirrors what arrived
CHAL_RESP	nonce(4)	echoed, at the responder's current counter
The reply's MIC proves the peer holds the group key; the echoed nonce proves it was generated now and isn't a recording. You set last_cnt from that frame's counter and the hole is closed permanently for that peer.

You don't buffer and re-evaluate the original command — just drop it. Your escalation ladder already retries, and the retry carries a counter higher than what the challenge established, so it's accepted normally. Cost is two extra frames, once per peer, only on the consequential path.

The teal branch matters as much as the coral one: replaying a stale temperature reading into a new node is noise, not an attack. Paying two frames of airtime to prevent it is the wrong trade. Reserve the challenge for CMD.

When a bulk sync is justified
Almost never — because a node doesn't need 255 counters, it needs counters for the peers it will accept commands from. That's usually one or two: the bridge, maybe a controller. Everything else is inbound telemetry it treats optimistically.

If you do want it (a node rejoining after months offline, wanting a warm neighbour table), do it as a nonce-protected request to the bridge, which has the most complete and freshest table since it hears everything:

node → bridge : SYNC_REQ  { nonce(4) }
bridge → node : SYNC_RESP { nonce(4), n(1), [addr(1) cnt(3)] × n }
Sixteen entries fit in a 64-byte body, so a full table is 16 frames — several minutes at SF9 under duty cycle. Fine as an occasional maintenance operation, wrong as a boot-time default.

Persistence and flash wear
Your own TX counter must survive power loss or you reuse a CCM nonce. Write it every 1000 sends with a +1000 forward jump on boot — around 16k flash writes over a device lifetime, comfortable for any MCU.

The RX table is different: losing it costs you a challenge exchange per peer, not a security break. So persist it opportunistically — on graceful shutdown, or every 15 minutes, or only the handful of entries for peers you accept commands from. Don't write 1 kB on every received frame.

One thing that falls out nicely: because counters only ever increase, a captured frame always carries a lower counter than what you'll have seen by the time it's replayed. There's no forgery path that produces a high counter, since the MIC covers it. The design has exactly one hole — the cold entry — and the challenge closes it.

