<result>{"odid-messages":{"verdict":"has-errors","corrections":["§0.3 reason #2 (unaligned multi-byte loads) is factually wrong on the exact targets it names. The report claims 'Latitude sits at byte offset 5 ... On ESP32-C3 (RISC-V) an unaligned word load traps into an emulation handler (slow) or faults depending on config; on Xtensa it faults.' __attribute__((__packed__)) sets the struct's alignment to 1 and marks every member as potentially unaligned, so the compiler emits byte-wise loads automatically. Verified by compiling a packed struct with an int32_t at offset 5 using the project's own toolchains: riscv32-esp-elf-gcc 8.4.0 emits four `lbu` instructions and xtensa-esp32s3-elf-gcc 8.4.0 emits four `l8ui` instructions — never a word load. FIX: replaced the rationale with the three reasons that are actually true (implementation-defined bitfield allocation order, strict-aliasing violation when casting a uint8_t* radio buffer to the struct type, and host-byte-order dependence of the multi-byte members), and added the disassembly evidence. The recommendation to use explicit shifts/masks is unchanged and still correct.","§4.3 table row for `SpeedMult == 1` states the range covered as '63.75 .. 255.00 m/s'. That upper bound is the invalid sentinel, not a valid speed. FIX: valid range is 63.75 .. 254.25 m/s (raw 0..254); raw 255 decodes to exactly 255.0 m/s and is the invalid/unknown value. Confirmed by encoding 254.25 (-&gt; mult=1 raw=254) and INV_SPEED_H (-&gt; mult=1 raw=255), and by encodeLocationMessage rejecting any input in (254.25, 255.0).","§1 asserts 'Value 2 | ASTM F3411 v1.1 second ballot draft = **ASTM F3411-22a**' as established fact. The library's own header comment reads 'ASTM F3411-v1.1 draft sent for second ballot round Q1 2022. (ASTM F3411-22 ?)' — with an explicit question mark — and the identical comment appears in the Android receiver's Constants.java. Both ASTM F3411-22 and F3411-22a are published editions (F3411-22a approved 2022-06-17). FIX: downgraded to what the sources actually say and moved the exact edition mapping to the still-open list.","§12 labels Timestamp accuracy values 1-15 as '`&lt;0.1 s` … `&lt;1.5 s`'. The second implementation renders these inclusively — LocationData.getTimeAccuracyAsString formats '&lt;= %1.1f s' — unlike every other accuracy enum, which renders '&lt; bound'. FIX: changed to `≤ 0.1 s` … `≤ 1.5 s` and flagged it as the one accuracy enum with an inclusive bound.","OMISSION (added): the BLE transport offset, which is the single fact needed to locate the 25 bytes in a received advertisement. Verified in OpenDroneIdDataManager.receiveDataBluetooth, which calls parseData(data, 6, ...), and in BluetoothScanner: the AD payload is `len | 0x16 (Service Data - 16-bit UUID) | FA FF (0xFFFA, ASTM Remote ID) | 0x0D (ODID app code) | message counter | 25-byte message`, so the message starts at offset 6 and the counter is at offset 5. Wi-Fi NAN and Beacon use offset 1 (counter at byte 0), matching `struct ODID_service_info { uint8_t message_counter; ODID_MessagePack_encoded odid_message_pack[]; }` in odid_wifi.h.","OMISSION (added): the Message Pack case in decodeOpenDroneID() is a buffer overread in the reference library, not merely a recursion hazard. The function documents that msgData points to 25 bytes, but for type 0xF it casts to ODID_MessagePack_encoded* (228 bytes) and reads up to 3 + 25*MsgPackSize. On an ESP32 fed from a radio buffer this reads past the end before any of the library's own checks run. The report's gate code handles it but never explains why the length check must precede the cast.","OMISSION (added): resolved the report's own open uncertainty about bitfield allocation on ESP32. Compiled a struct with the exact header/BasicID/Location/System byte-1 bitfield shapes and a constant initializer for both riscv32-esp-elf-gcc and xtensa-esp32s3-elf-gcc; both emit .byte 18, 18, 39, 6 (0x12, 0x12, 0x27, 0x06), identical to arm64 clang and to the encoder's own output. LSb-first allocation is confirmed on both real ESP32 targets, not just assumed.","OMISSION (added): decodeSystemMessage() performs no field validation whatsoever and decodeSelfIDMessage() does not validate DescType, so every reserved and out-of-range enum value reaches the caller unchanged. Consistent with the report's (correct) 'do not reject on reserved values' policy, but an implementer needs to know the reference decoder provides no filtering here.","OMISSION (added): decodeAuthMessage()'s `Length &gt; MAX_AUTH_LENGTH` check is inside `#if (MAX_AUTH_LENGTH &lt; UINT8_MAX)`. At the default ODID_AUTH_MAX_PAGES=16, MAX_AUTH_LENGTH is 362 (measured) so that check is compiled out entirely; only the `capacity &lt; Length` test runs. The report presented both as unconditional.","OMISSION (added): the Android receiver's page-0 Authentication failure policy differs from the C library's. On `authLastPageIndex &gt;= 16 || authLength &gt; len` it zeroes LastPageIndex/Length/Timestamp and keeps the message, where the C library rejects it outright. Worth stating since the report leans on the two implementations agreeing.","CLARIFICATION (added): the ODID_status enum's header comment says '3 to 15 reserved', which is stale — 3 (Emergency) and 4 (Remote ID System Failure) are both defined. The report's table (5-15 reserved) is correct; noted explicitly so an implementer reading the header does not 'correct' the table back.","OMISSION (added): opendroneid.h also declares an unrelated FRDID_UAS_Data struct and frdid_build/frdid_wifi_build_beacon_frame functions (French Direct ID). These share the header but have nothing to do with the ODID 25-byte message format and must not be confused with it. Also added ODID_MessagePack_data (SingleMessageSize + MsgPackSize + Messages[9]), the non-encoded pack struct encodeMessagePack() takes."],"findings":"# Open Drone ID — byte-exact message specification\n\nNormative source: `opendroneid/opendroneid-core-c` @ commit `6484f26545d4f012682524e2d843fab0fbdc0b34` (branch `master`, verified 2026-09-08 via the GitHub API; commit \"Merge pull request #110 from friissoren/bug_fix2\", authored 2026-09-08T15:32:56Z).\n\n**The directory is `libopendroneid/`, not `libraries/opendroneid/`.** Raw URLs are listed at the end.\n\nEverything below was **verified empirically, twice**: the library was compiled and its encoder output dumped for known inputs, and every extraction was cross-checked against the Android receiver's independent hand-written parser (`OpenDroneIdParser.java`), which agrees on every field. Bitfield allocation was additionally confirmed on the **real ESP32-C3 (RISC-V) and ESP32-S3 (Xtensa) GCC toolchains**, not merely on a host compiler.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Global facts\n\n| Fact | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Message size | **exactly 25 bytes** (`ODID_MESSAGE_SIZE`) |\n| Endianness | **little-endian** for all multi-byte integers — confirmed (§0.2) |\n| Struct packing | `__attribute__((__packed__))`, no padding; every encoded message struct `sizeof` == 25 (measured) |\n| Message Pack struct | 228 bytes = 3 + 25×9 (measured) |\n| Current protocol version emitted | 2 (`ODID_PROTOCOL_VERSION`) |\n| `ODID_ID_SIZE` | 20 |\n| `ODID_STR_SIZE` | 23 |\n| `ODID_AUTH_PAGE_ZERO_DATA_SIZE` | 17 |\n| `ODID_AUTH_PAGE_NONZERO_DATA_SIZE` | 23 |\n| `ODID_AUTH_MAX_PAGES` | 16 (compile-time, settable 1–16) |\n| `ODID_BASIC_ID_MAX_MESSAGES` | 2 (compile-time, settable 1–5) |\n| `ODID_PACK_MAX_MESSAGES` | 9 |\n| `MAX_AUTH_LENGTH` | 362 (measured) = 17 + 23×15 |\n\n### 0.1 Measured reference dumps\n\nReal output of `encodeXxxMessage()`, independently reproduced. These are the ground truth for everything in this document.\n\n```\nsizes: basic=25 loc=25 authz=25 authn=25 self=25 sys=25 op=25 pack=228\n\nbasicID   02 12 41 42 43 44 31 32 33 34 35 36 37 38 39 30 31 32 33 34 35 00 00 00 00\nlocation  12 27 14 30 F6 C8 57 BA 1B 38 02 F4 03 B8 0B E0 0B 98 08 4B 33 9F 8C 05 00\nsystem    42 06 40 56 B7 1B 80 14 EF 03 01 00 32 A0 0F D0 07 24 F0 0A 4
