<result>{"esp32-spectrum":{"verdict":"has-errors","corrections":["FABRICATED CAUSAL LINK (most serious). The report states pm_noise_check 'is gated behind a modulo of a power-management tick counter against NoiseTimerInterval, which I read out of pp.o's .data.NoiseTimerInterval as 0x64 = 100.' Two separately-true facts were welded into a false claim. Verified: NoiseTimerInterval is defined in libpp.a:pp.o as a 2-byte D symbol whose contents are `64 00` = 100 (confirmed by objdump -s -j .data.NoiseTimerInterval). But `nm -u` across every library in the SDK finds NO undefined reference to it, and `objdump -r pp.o` shows its ONLY relocation is in section .xt.prop — the Xtensa linker property/relaxation section, not executable code. pm_noise_check lives in pm.o and has no relocation to it at all; its modulo (`rems a2, a8, a2`) divides a g_pm tick field by a quotient built from g_pm+52 and the literal 0x00040000. Consequence: the refresh cadence of the noise-floor cache CANNOT be stated in seconds from this binary. The report's '~100 PM ticks, i.e. on the order of seconds, not the ~100 us you need' must be withdrawn and replaced with 'periodic and tick-gated, period not determined.'","MISREAD OF THE KEY LITERATURE'S ANTENNA CONFIGURATION. The report says the arXiv 2606.07347 result's 'whole processing gain comes from inter-antenna phase averaging over 3 RX chains.' The paper's own setup table (verified) says 'MIMO 3x1 (AP x STA)' — three TRANSMIT chains at the Buffalo AP, ONE receive chain at the Raspberry Pi CM4. The averaging is over three transmit chains observed on one receiver. The corrected version of this argument is stronger, not weaker: those three chains share one AP oscillator and are therefore mutually phase-coherent, which is exactly why three independent ESP32 transmitters could not substitute for them.","esp_private/wifi.h contains 37 esp_wifi_* function declarations, not the 42 claimed. (Conclusion unaffected — enumerated in full, nothing measurement-related; the only power-adjacent names are esp_wifi_power_domain_on/off, which gate the power domain, and esp_wifi_internal_get_negotiated_bandwidth/channel.)","libphy.a exports 259 defined (T/D/B/R) symbols, not 272.","phy.h declares 14 names, not the 9 listed. The report's list omits coex_bt_high_prio, coexist, phy_eco_version_sel, phy_freq_mem_backup, phy_get_romfunc_addr and phy_init_flag, and wrongly places get_phy_version_str in phy.h — it is declared in esp_phy_init.h:246. The conclusion (no measurement function declared) survives on the corrected list.","THE THREE MOST PLAUSIBLE-SOUNDING libphy CANDIDATES WERE NEVER EXAMINED. The report lists meas_tone_pwr_db, get_power_db and phy_get_rx_gain as existing and then silently drops them. Disassembled here: meas_tone_pwr_db (phy_pwdet.o) calls start_tx_tone_step, loops four times over get_power_db, then stop_tx_tone — it KEYS THE TRANSMITTER and measures its own output. get_power_db reads the power-detector SAR via get_fm_sar_dout through a g_phyFuns vtable slot; the whole object (get_tone_sar_dout, txtone_linear_pwr, pwdet_tone_start, phy_set_pwdet_power, ram_read_sar2_code) is the closed-loop TX power calibration path, not an RX detector. phy_get_rx_gain is a gain-table helper and phy_force_rx_gain is a pure setter (calls rom_rx_gain_force). This omission is a safety issue: an experimenter poking meas_tone_pwr_db would transmit.","'Even at 2.4 GHz you have one 20 MHz window' is wrong. The datasheet feature list states '802.11n MCS0-7 that supports 20 MHz and 40 MHz bandwidth' and Table 6-4 lists HT40 sensitivities down to -90.0 dBm (MCS0). The window is 20 or 40 MHz.","'one antenna' needs qualifying. 1T1R (one RF chain) is correct and is the load-bearing fact, but it is stated in the datasheet's Section 1 feature list ('1T1R mode with data rate up to 150 Mbps'), not in Section 6.1 as cited, and the S3 does support antenna DIVERSITY through an external RF switch — a second antenna selectable onto the same single chain, which does not provide a second coherent chain.","DJI O4 lower 5 GHz band is 5.150-5.250 GHz per DJI's own Goggles 3 specification page, not the 5.170-5.250 GHz stated. The '60 MHz occupied bandwidth' figure does not appear on DJI's published specification and is unsourced; it has been removed and the sourced transmit-power and band figures substituted.","'the driver's own passive-scan default dwell is 360 ms/channel' is unsourced and could not be reproduced. It is not in esp_wifi_types.h, not in the framework sdkconfig, and not in the IDF v4.4.6 wifi.rst (which documents a 120 ms default for ACTIVE scans only). Only the '&gt;1500 ms may cause station to disconnect' warning on wifi_scan_time_t.passive is verifiable. Every derived sweep-rate figure has been marked as an unmeasured estimate.","ic_get_rssi does not subtract the global rssi_margin symbol. Disassembly: it calls rc_get_trc(ifidx, ...), and on success returns peer[3] - wDevCtrl[46]; on lookup failure it returns the constant 31. (Conclusion unchanged: cached per-station RSSI, not an energy detector.)","esp8266/Arduino#2386 is CLOSED, not 'unanswered'. Verified title 'WiFi: Signal to Noise Ratio (SNR) and/or noise measurement', opened by bitnapper 2016-08-11. The defensible statement is that it was closed without such an API being added and none exists in the core today.","first_word_invalid is not documented as an ESP32-S3-specific limitation. The IDF v4.4.6 docs source reads 'due to a hardware limitation in {IDF_TARGET_NAME}' — a build-time template substitution, so the same sentence renders for every target.","Struct line citations drift. In the vendored esp_wifi_types.h: wifi_pkt_rx_ctrl_t is 381-428 (not 380-428), WIFI_PKT_MISC is line 447 (not 444), the WIFI_PROMIS_FILTER_MASK_* block is 451-458 (not 447-456), and wifi_csi_config_t / wifi_csi_info_t are 482-504.","The CSI total-byte set is 128/256/376/380/384/612; the report omitted 376 (HT, 40 MHz, STBC, secondary channel above).","The stated reason Bluedroid HCI helpers 'are not even in the link' is wrong. The framework's sdkconfig has CONFIG_BT_BLUEDROID_ENABLED=y and libbt.a exports every symbol named (btsnd_hcic_ble_receiver_test, btsnd_hcic_ble_enhand_rx_test, BTM_BleGetEnergyInfo, BTA_DmBleGetEnergyInfo, btsnd_hcic_read_rssi, BTM_ReadRSSI — all confirmed T). The correct reason is that unreferenced archive members are not pulled in, and this firmware's host stack is the NimBLE-Arduino ^1.3.8 library. The symbols remain available to anyone who calls them.","The '~30x below one quantization step' figure rests on an unstated, unsourced assumption of |H| ~ 40 LSB. Restated with the assumption declared and the conclusion shown to be robust across the whole plausible range (6x to 30x below one LSB for |H| from 180 down to 40).","wDev_GetNoiseFloor is understated as 'a cache'. The disassembly shows it re-reads hardware only when the cached byte at wDevCtrl+47 is &gt;= 1; a valid (negative dBm) cached value short-circuits, so REPEATED CALLS RETURN A CONSTANT. The pollable entry point is rom_read_hw_noisefloor, not this one.","The claimed empirical link test (probe TU built with `pio run -e droneTracker`, SUCCESS in 34.26 s) was not re-run here and no probe file exists in the tree. It is unnecessary: rom_phy_get_noisefloor and rom_read_hw_noisefloor are unconditional absolute assignments (not PROVIDE) in esp32s3.rom.ld, and wDev_GetNoiseFloor is a T symbol in libpp.a which is on the link line, so resolution is guaranteed by construction. Restated on that basis.","SX126x getRSSI(false) is described as 'pollable at kHz rates' with no source and without the operating precondition. GetRssiInst is only meaningful with the radio in RX; the poll rate is an SPI-bound estimate, now marked as such.","Remote ID evasion is asserted flatly ('A contraband-delivery operator disables it') with no reporting behind it. Reporting is mixed: firmware modification to suppress Remote ID is described as complex and liable to brick or trip fail
